<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hailu Tekle Haymanot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hailu Tekle Haymanot was an Ethiopian nobleman, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-of-gojjam">Tekle Haymanot of Gojjam</a>, who governed Gojjam with the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> and became one of the richest lords of the empire. In 1932 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> arrested him for helping the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a> escape, and he lost his province. He later collaborated with the Italian occupation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hailu was a son of Negus Tekle Haymanot Tessemma of Gojjam, long a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. When his father died on 10 January 1901, three of the sons fought over the succession, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> instead divided the province into three parts under governors of his own. In 1908 his daughter Sabla Wangel Hailu married Menelik's grandson and heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a>, and in 1909 he married Assalafetch Wolde Hanna, a cousin of Taytu Betul, the empress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As ruler of Gojjam, according to the historian Gebru Tareke, Hailu introduced new taxes, auctioned political and church offices, nearly monopolised the province's export trade and enlarged his estates in disregard of customary land allocation, which earned him the nickname birru, or dollars, Hailu. In 1924 he accompanied Ras Tafari Makonnen on his European tour, was given an honorary British knighthood, the KBE, and afterwards remodelled his father's palace at Debre Marqos on European lines.</p><p>When Tafari became Emperor Haile Selassie I in 1930, Hailu held on to the customs and tax revenues that other great lords gave up, and the historian Harold Marcus records that he sought favours from the American and British legations, hinting that he could block their access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>. He was fined heavily and lost half his property in 1932, and after the escape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a> a tribunal convicted him of mendacity, corruption, tax evasion and treason. Ras Imru Haile Selassie replaced him in Gojjam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 2 May 1936, leaving for exile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> had Hailu taken from prison on his train, but released him at Dire Dawa; Hailu returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> and submitted to the Italians, who gave him the Star of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-saints-maurice-and-lazarus">Order of Saints Maurice and Lazarus</a>. After a failed Ethiopian attack on Addis Ababa in July 1936 he assured two of its commanders, Aberra Kassa and Asfawossen Kassa, that they would not be harmed; they surrendered at Fiche on 21 December, and the Italians executed them.</p><p>The Italians returned him to power in Gojjam only at the very end of their occupation. In 1941 he handed Gojjam over to the returning emperor, lived confined to the capital with the dignities of a senior prince, and was given a state funeral on his death in 1950. In the view of Gebru Tareke, his death finished off the provincial ruling elite, which had been grudgingly yielding ground to the centralists since the closing decade of the nineteenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hailu Tekle Haymanot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Ras Hailu</b> Tekle Haymanot later ruled Gojjam as one of the empire's wealthiest lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gojjam">Kingdom of Gojjam</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.04,38.75&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Addis Ababa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hailu Tekle Haymanot"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5736 4230h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5736 4230h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=9.04,38.75&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
